{"title":"Adding fractions with different denominators","key_idea":"To add fractions with different bottom numbers, you must first make the bottom numbers the same, then add the tops.","method":["Find the smallest number that both denominators divide into evenly — this is called the least common multiple.","Change each fraction so it has that common denominator by multiplying the top and bottom by the same number.","Add the new tops together and keep the common denominator the same."],"worked_example":{"problem":"Add 2/5 and 1/10.","steps":["The denominators are 5 and 10; the least common multiple is 10.","Change 2/5 to have denominator 10: multiply top and bottom by 2, giving 4/10.","Keep 1/10 as it is.","Add the tops: 4 + 1 = 5, so the answer is 5/10.","Simplify: 5/10 = 1/2."],"answer":"1/2"},"key_terms":[{"term":"denominator","meaning":"the bottom number of a fraction"},{"term":"least common multiple","meaning":"the smallest number that two denominators both divide into evenly"},{"term":"equivalent fractions","meaning":"fractions that look different but are worth the same amount"}],"common_mistakes":["Adding the tops and bottoms together instead of making the bottoms equal first.","Forgetting to multiply both the top and bottom by the same number."],"remember":"Make the bottoms the same before you add the tops.","quick_check":[{"q":"Add 1/6 and 1/3.","a":"1/2"},{"q":"Add 3/5 and 2/10.","a":"4/5"}],"tell_your_child":"First find what bottom number they both fit into, then change both fractions so they match that bottom. Then just add the tops!","picture":{"concept_key":"c5-math-fraction-addition-unlike-denominators","scene_prompt":"Two children sharing pizza slices of different sizes, cutting slices into matching pieces before combining them on one plate."}}
